##########################################################################
#                                                                        #
#                           SPECTRAL_ENGINE                              #
#                                                                        #
##########################################################################

Real-time spectral analysis and resynthesis engine currently supporting macOS/Linux for x86 & ARM based architectures. 
Intentionally targets ARM Cortex-M/A targets with acceleration support available for:

[✓] Metal & CUDA platforms
[✓] ARM, X86
[✓] Linux (fftw3), MacOS (vDSP)
[✓] SIMD support via. SIMDE
[✓] OpenMP

With support eventually planned for the following:

[X] OpenGL / Vulkan (GPU acceleration framework)
[X] RISC-V          (Full support)
[X] FreeRTOS        (For embedded platforms)
[X] OpenBSD         (Full support)

If you are interested in a more detailed project description see spectral_engine/README.md

------------
DEPENDENCIES
------------

A pinned, reproducible environment with EVERY dependency below (except the
macOS system SDK, the optional CUDA stack, and the external Daisy SDKs) is
provided by the Nix flake at the repo root:

    nix develop            # drops you into a shell with the full toolchain

The full list, by role:

CORE BUILD (always required)
- CMake ≥ 3.21
- GNU Make (the root Makefile wraps cmake; you can also call cmake directly)
- A C11 compiler — Clang (Apple/LLVM) or GCC
- OpenMP runtime — libomp (macOS/Clang) or libgomp (Linux/GCC); CPU parallel synth
- libsndfile — WAV/audio I/O

PLATFORM ACCELERATION (auto-selected by the build)
- macOS: Accelerate / vDSP (FFT + atan2) and Metal + Foundation (GPU backend).
  These ship with the Xcode Command Line Tools / macOS SDK — no separate install.
- Linux: FFTW3 (libfftw3-dev) for the host FFT.
- Optional GPU (Linux + NVIDIA): CUDA Toolkit / nvcc — build with
  `-DSPECTRAL_USE_CUDA=ON` and target `cuda`.

VENDORED, IN-SOURCE (git submodules; managed by `python -m spectral_tools.vendor`)
- CMSIS-DSP   — Q15/Q31 FFT for the IFFT synthesis path
- CMSIS_6     — Cortex-M core headers
- simde       — header-only SIMD portability (SSE2 intrinsics -> NEON/AVX)
- xxHash      — resource hash system (xx32/xx3)
  See `third_party/libs.yaml` for the manifest; patchsets are kept in-source as subtrees.

EMBEDDED + ARM PERFORMANCE MODEL (Cortex-M firmware, perf gate, WCET)
- arm-none-eabi-gcc ≥ 15 with newlib — VENDORED in-tree at
  `tools/toolchains/xpack-arm-none-eabi-gcc-15.2.1` (the perf stack uses it by
  default; a system/brew install also works).
- qemu-system-arm ≥ 11 — the [measured] M7 instruction counts.
- llvm-mca (from LLVM) — the [modeled] M7 cycle numbers (the perf gate's modeled
  half). On macOS Homebrew this keg is keg-only; the discovery checks
  `/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin/llvm-mca` directly, so no PATH change is needed.
- dfu-util — `make daisy-flash` (flashing a Daisy Seed).
- libDaisy + DaisySP — external board SDKs for `make daisy`, passed via
  `-DSPECTRAL_DAISY_LIBDAISY_DIR=... -DSPECTRAL_DAISY_DAISYSP_DIR=...`.
  (llvm-mca and qemu are ONLY needed for the embedded perf model / `pytest tests/tools`;
   the desktop build does not require them.)

PYTHON ≥ 3.12 (tooling: vendoring, perf model, benchmarks, behavioral tests)
- exact pins in `tools/requirements.txt`: numpy 2.4.2, xxhash 3.6.0,
  pytest 8.4.1, pyyaml 6.0.3.  `pip install -r tools/requirements.txt`

OPTIONAL DEV TOOLS (build degrades gracefully if absent)
- ripgrep (rg)     — the log-discipline build check
- llvm-profdata    — PGO profile merge (also from LLVM)

QUICK INSTALL (outside Nix)
- macOS:  ```brew install cmake libomp libsndfile llvm qemu dfu-util ripgrep```
          (plus Xcode Command Line Tools for Accelerate/Metal)
- Debian/Ubuntu:
          ```sudo apt-get install -y cmake build-essential libomp-dev \
              libsndfile1-dev libfftw3-dev qemu-system-arm gcc-arm-none-eabi \
              llvm dfu-util ripgrep```
- Python (any OS):  ```pip install -r tools/requirements.txt```

BUILD

Run all build commands from the repository root.

Native build
```
  make configure
  make
```

CUDA desktop build (Linux + nvcc; use target `cuda` to build the desktop CUDA binary)
```
  make configure CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DSPECTRAL_USE_CUDA=ON'
  make cuda
```

Embedded-target builds
```
make configure CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DSPECTRAL_DAISY_LIBDAISY_DIR=/path/to/libDaisy -DSPECTRAL_DAISY_DAISYSP_DIR=/path/to/DaisySP'
make embedded_arm_restricted   # host build with embedded constraints
make daisy                     # Cortex-M firmware build (Daisy Seed)
```

The currently supported workflow on supported embedded devices is to build the segment binary offline & then either bake it into the firmware or upload it onto the device's flash. E.g,
```
make configure
make desktop daisy
./build/bin/spectral_*_desktop ./resources/testing/shakespeare_he_saw_the_cat.wav <...> 4
make daisy-flash       # Flash the target device with the segment binary
```

An overview of the build system:
```
make CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DSPECTRAL_DEBUG_LOGGING=ON'  # Turn on debug logging

make clean && make clean-output && make CMAKE_CONFIGURE_ARGS='-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DSPECTRAL_PRODUCTION_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_REPRO_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_DEBUG_LOGGING=ON' # A standard run using the
intrinsics of the root makefile (preferred)

make clean && make clean-output && cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DSPECTRAL_PRODUCTION_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_REPRO_BUILD=OFF -DSPECTRAL_DEBUG_LOGGING=ON && cmake --build build/ --target desktop --parallel # A standard run using the full extrinsics of cmake, equiv. to above
```

Refer to:
```
make info
make help
```
for the full matrix/options.

USAGE

  ./build/bin/spectral_*_desktop input.wav [timbre] [stretch] [pitch] [n_fft] [hop] [thresh] [threads] [backend]

  Timbres: 0=sine 1=saw 2=square 3=tri 4=asin 5=para 6=quant 7=pwm
  Backends: 0=auto 1=cpu 2=metal 3=cuda 4=export

  Named options:
  -w <file>              Use wavetable file (.spwt/.bin/.raw/.hex)
  -pm, --proc-mask <s>   Processing chain mask (comma-separated names or integer bitmask)
  --cache                Prewarm backend and cache analyzed segments for this input
  -q, --quality <mode>   Anti-alias oscillator quality (CPU float path):
                         naive (default), polyblep, additive, oversample
  -S, --simd             CPU oscillator uses SIMD (default; ~1.8x on saw/square, <=1 ULP vs scalar)
  --scalar               Force the scalar reference oscillator (parity/debug)
  --q15                  Opt into the Q15 fixed-point compute domain (forces CPU backend):
                         packed 8-wide SIMD Q15 on saw/square/tri/para (~1.4-1.6x over
                         float-SIMD), scalar Q15 under --scalar; float is the default

STRUCTURE

  spectral_engine/    Device agnostic core analysis, synthesis and runtime utilities
  tests/              Self-explanitory
  api/                Platform-specific wrappers or ports
  examples/           Example applications & Demo's
  tools/              Useful scripts, toolings, workflows & devops
  resources/          Location for auxiliary files
  build/bin/          Compiled binaries

